{"content": "TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol) are two fundamental protocols of the Internet Protocol Suite (TCP/IP). They both operate at the **Transport Layer** (Layer 4) but differ significantly in how they handle data transmission.\n\nHere are the main differences between TCP and UDP:\n\n### 1. Connection-Oriented vs. Connectionless\n- **TCP**: **Connection-oriented**. Before data transfer begins, a connection is established between the sender and receiver using a **three-way handshake** (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK). This ensures both parties are ready to communicate.\n- **UDP**: **Connectionless**. No handshake is required. The sender simply sends data to the receiver\u2019s IP address and port. There is no guarantee the receiver is ready or", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "TCP", "logprob": -0.6228816220999036, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "TCP", "logprob": -0.6228816220999036}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -1.0697829435110364}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -3.3313567350149427}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -3.3491732785940442}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -4.673533172392872}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -4.884016723418263}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -5.0066611478567395}, {"token": "When", "logprob": -5.320730895781544}, {"token": "Transport", "logprob": -5.6088854978323255}, {"token": "Transmission", "logprob": -5.681448669219044}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -5.8846423337698255}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -5.8902270505667005}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -6.774214477324513}, {"token": "While", "logprob": -6.915638656401661}, {"token": "Both", "logprob": -7.3778063962698255}, {"token": "Network", "logprob": -7.657858581328419}, {"token": "Trans", "logprob": -8.47304126718047}, {"token": "There", "logprob": -8.535240859770802}, {"token": "Compar", "logprob": -8.572581023955372}, {"token": "Tcp", "logprob": -8.633843154692677}]}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -0.007475703169625092, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " (", "logprob": -0.007