{"content": "### What is a Merkle Tree?\n\nA **Merkle tree** (also known as a **Hash Tree**) is a data structure used in computer science, specifically in cryptography and distributed systems. It is a binary tree where each non-leaf node is the cryptographic hash of the concatenation of its two child nodes.\n\nThe structure was invented by Ralph Merkle in 1979.\n\n#### Core Structure\n1.  **Leaf Nodes**: These contain the raw data (e.g., a transaction, a file block, or a record). Each leaf is hashed to produce a \"leaf hash.\"\n2.  **Intermediate Nodes**: Each node at this level is the hash of the two child nodes below it.\n3.  **Root Node**: The single node", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "###", "logprob": -0.7680550965146828, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "###", "logprob": -0.7680550965146828}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.9504548462705422}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -2.445562497027378}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -3.0420361908750344}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -4.7291795166806985}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -6.247116223223667}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -6.358142987139683}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -6.892433300860386}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -7.866829052813511}, {"token": "What", "logprob": -8.057075634844761}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -8.679021969683628}, {"token": "Definition", "logprob": -9.350563183672886}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -9.854196682818394}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -9.896433010943394}, {"token": "M", "logprob": -10.210212841876011}, {"token": "Below", "logprob": -10.360448971636753}, {"token": "<think>", "logprob": -10.479223385699253}, {"token": "Since", "logprob": -10.596697941668491}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -10.701144352801304}, {"token": "At", "logprob": -10.845003262408238}]}, {"token": " What", "logprob": -0.43430963710495263, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " What", "logprob": -0.43430963710495263}, {"token": " Definition", "logprob": -1.5222708340043667}, {"token": " Logical", "logprob": -3.3595606441850308}, 