{"content": "In computer science, both **processes** and **threads** are fundamental units of execution, but they differ significantly in how they manage resources, memory, and isolation.\n\nHere is a detailed explanation of the differences between a process and a thread.\n\n### 1. Definition\n\n*   **Process**: A process is an instance of a computer program that is being executed. It is the basic unit of resource allocation in an operating system. Each process has its own unique memory space (address space), including its own code, data, and stack.\n*   **Thread**: A thread (often called a \"lightweight process\") is a single flow of control within a process. A process can contain multiple threads. All threads within a process share the same memory space and resources.\n\n###", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "In", "logprob": -0.7617907493736453, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "In", "logprob": -0.7617907493736453}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -0.785262581649036}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -3.609797951522083}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -3.9402365654136844}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -4.900342461409778}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -5.165896889510364}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -5.266406533065052}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -5.474015709700794}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -6.483447548690052}, {"token": "While", "logprob": -6.647593972029895}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -6.656209465804309}, {"token": "Understanding", "logprob": -6.936082360091419}, {"token": "When", "logprob": -7.218516823592395}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -7.4755854576255985}, {"token": "At", "logprob": -7.9204096763755985}, {"token": "Both", "logprob": -9.012521263900013}, {"token": "An", "logprob": -9.132068154158802}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -9.152049538436145}, {"token": "Processes", "logprob": -9.281985756697864}, {"token": "Process", "logprob": -9.466136452498645}]}, {"token": " computer", "logprob": -0.3824723568313857, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " computer", "logprob": -0.3