{"content": "In the context of machine learning and information retrieval, **Precision** and **Recall** are two metrics used to evaluate the performance of a classification model. They measure different aspects of the model's ability to identify positive cases.\n\nHere is the step-by-step breakdown of the differences, followed by a concrete example.\n\n### 1. Definitions and Formulas\n\nTo understand the difference, we must first define the four outcomes of a classification:\n*   **True Positive (TP):** The model predicted \"Yes,\" and the answer was \"Yes.\"\n*   **False Positive (FP):** The model predicted \"Yes,\" but the answer was \"No\" (False Alarm).\n*   **False Negative (FN):** The model predicted \"No,\" but the answer was \"Yes\" (", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "In", "logprob": -0.3398451951909358, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "In", "logprob": -0.3398451951909358}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -2.157817378418475}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -2.2070231584477717}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -3.264178767578631}, {"token": "Precision", "logprob": -4.738465800659686}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -5.364890590088397}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -5.375403895752459}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -6.074409022705584}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -6.391196742432147}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -7.441031947510272}, {"token": "Both", "logprob": -8.128274455444842}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -8.36210586108449}, {"token": "These", "logprob": -8.905795588867694}, {"token": "Prec", "logprob": -9.124621882813006}, {"token": "Logic", "logprob": -9.495854869263201}, {"token": "When", "logprob": -9.739581599609881}, {"token": "Math", "logprob": -9.74971915759328}, {"token": "Since", "logprob": -9.889804377930194}, {"token": "While", "logprob": -10.339957728760272}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -10.490046992676287}]}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.7881765235428091, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " the", "logprob": -0.7881765235428091}, {"token": " machine", "logprob"