{"content": "### Explanation of Big-O Notation\n\n**Big-O notation** is a mathematical notation used in computer science to describe the **upper bound** of the growth rate of an algorithm's time or space complexity.\n\nIn simple terms, it answers the question: **\"As the input size ($n$) grows, how does the performance of this algorithm degrade?\"**\n\nIt focuses on the **worst-case scenario** and ignores constant factors and lower-order terms. For example, if an algorithm takes $3n^2 + 5n + 10$ steps, Big-O notation simplifies this to $O(n^2)$ because the $n^2$ term dominates the growth as $n$ becomes very large.\n\n---\n\n### Two Examples\n\n#### Example 1: Linear", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "###", "logprob": -0.3612448480067343, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "###", "logprob": -0.3612448480067343}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -1.9930159356532187}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -2.532463720077047}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -2.831410100692281}, {"token": "Big", "logprob": -3.992006948226461}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -5.650395086043844}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -6.1624914910731405}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -6.773125341170797}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -6.9062048699793905}, {"token": "What", "logprob": -7.7404238488612265}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -7.848408391707906}, {"token": "Explanation", "logprob": -8.901350667708883}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -9.024630239242086}, {"token": "An", "logprob": -9.270725896590719}, {"token": "Ex", "logprob": -9.289623906844625}, {"token": "Definition", "logprob": -10.153443982833394}, {"token": "Below", "logprob": -10.182526281112203}, {"token": "As", "logprob": -10.18662708067275}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -10.333639791244039}, {"token": "Understanding", "logprob": -10.35890262388564}]}, {"token": " Explanation", "logprob": -0.8747006053745991, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " Explanation", "logprob": -0.8747006053745991}, {"token": " What", "logprob": -1.3511887187779195}, {"token": " Understanding", "l