{"content": "Vaccines train the immune system through a process known as **immunization**. This process mimics a natural infection without causing the disease itself, allowing the body to build a \"memory\" of the pathogen.\n\nHere is a step-by-step breakdown of how this training works:\n\n### 1. Introduction of the Antigen\nA vaccine contains a specific part of a pathogen (virus or bacteria) called an **antigen**. This antigen is designed to be recognized by the immune system but is usually weakened, inactivated, or a fragment of the pathogen, so it does not cause severe illness.\n*   **Examples of antigens:**\n    *   **Live-attenuated:** A weakened version of the live virus (e.g., MMR vaccine).\n", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "V", "logprob": -0.400271880931939, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "V", "logprob": -0.400271880931939}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -1.6350016477776421}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -3.030032623119439}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -3.370334136791314}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -4.3109621885491265}, {"token": "When", "logprob": -5.278998840160455}, {"token": "Think", "logprob": -5.327170837230767}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -5.419463622875298}, {"token": "How", "logprob": -5.593858230419244}, {"token": "Vac", "logprob": -6.0339541319085015}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -6.354888427562798}, {"token": "Modern", "logprob": -6.498202789135064}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -6.672280776805962}, {"token": "Understanding", "logprob": -6.810580718822564}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -6.830958831615533}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -6.904927718944634}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -6.952397811718072}, {"token": " Vacc", "logprob": -7.30643414289483}, {"token": "Training", "logprob": -7.469993102855767}, {"token": "<think>", "logprob": -7.504960525341119}]}, {"token": "acc", "logprob": -0.005341063077406005, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "acc", "logprob": -0.005341063077406005}, {"token": "act", "logprob": -5.761738310391859}, {"token": " vaccines"