{"content": "To prove that $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational, we will use the method of **proof by contradiction**.\n\n### Step 1: Assumption\nAssume the opposite of what we want to prove. Assume that $\\sqrt{2}$ is a **rational** number.\n\nBy definition, a rational number can be expressed as a fraction $\\frac{a}{b}$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers, $b \\neq 0$, and the fraction is in its **simplest form** (i.e., $a$ and $b$ share no common factors other than 1; they are coprime).\n\nSo, let:\n$$ \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{a}{b} $$\nwhere $\\gcd(a,", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "To", "logprob": -0.007585388825802397, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "To", "logprob": -0.007585388825802397}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -5.701619965241818}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -6.433898789094357}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -7.015891892121701}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -7.50427423159924}, {"token": "We", "logprob": -7.736724716828732}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -8.883075577424435}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -9.448131424592404}, {"token": "There", "logprob": -9.613780838654904}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -9.756624085114865}, {"token": "Proof", "logprob": -9.838809830354123}, {"token": "Ass", "logprob": -10.188522202180295}, {"token": "One", "logprob": -10.444255692170529}, {"token": "Standard", "logprob": -10.706889969514279}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -10.92949281375256}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -10.976833206818966}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -11.249124390290646}, {"token": "Below", "logprob": -11.386947495149045}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -11.49588857333508}, {"token": "Classic", "logprob": -11.520239693330197}]}, {"token": " prove", "logprob": -3.592686286779667e-05, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " prove", "logprob": -3.592686286779667e-05}, {"token": " demonstrate", "logprob": -11.39745108799568}, {"token": " provide", "logprob": -12.183801338239821}, {"token": " proof", "logprob": -12.48532073216072}, {"token": " Prove", "logprob": -12.876126930280837}, {"token": "pr